WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:18.140
NHS England Corporate and NHS Performance Report

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.260
Karen Wheeler: I am

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:30.029
not going to say too much about the programmes
beyond what is covered in the paper. I did

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:38.550
want to just make the point that we are, as
ever, keeping all of our programmes under

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:45.510
close scrutiny to make sure that we are confident
that they will deliver successfully for us.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:52.610
As we are currently working on the way that
we operate, we are looking at the extent to

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:59.579
which those programmes can particularly help
and support STPs and the transformation of

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:05.600
the NHS, and make sure that we focus all programmes
appropriately on that.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.600
I did, though, want to say a few words about

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:17.700
primary care services, given the conversation
that we have had at recent Boards about the

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:24.050
issues that have been created as a result
of problems in primary care services since

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:32.500
it was contracted out to Capita. I am pleased
to be able to assure the Board that we are,

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:39.460
with Capita, making very significant progress
on recovering and stabilising the services.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:49.470
There are still issues, and Capita are working
hard to address those issues, so we are not

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:55.320
completely where we need to be, but all of
the individual services that are provided

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:05.220
by Capita now have recovery plans, and we
are confident that most of those services

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:11.500
will be very largely recovered by the end
of March, and indeed, some of those services

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:21.360
are already working at acceptable levels.
The areas where we continue to have to do

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:29.300
some more work and which may take a bit longer
to fully recover are in relation to the Performers

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:37.720
List, and ensuring that practitioners, especially
those who are newly qualified, are fully on

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:45.560
the Performers List. Again, this is a very
high priority for the work that we are doing

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:51.000
and that Capita are doing to ensure that we
do get all of our performers as quickly as

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:58.320
possible onto the Performers List, and we
know that is a big concern for GPs, for dentists,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:06.380
and optometrists, in particular. I just really
wanted to provide that level of assurance

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:15.250
for the Board, so that you could be confident
that we will achieve a successful recovery

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.130
in the very near future, and that is all I
really wanted to say.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:33.360
Joanne Shaw: I think we just want to welcome
that, Karen, and to show our appreciation

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.960
for the good work that has been done. You
have obviously focused on ameliorating the

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:43.790
position for GPs, other health professionals,
their colleagues and their patients, who have

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:49.230
been impacted by this, so it is very good
to hear that that is going in the right direction.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:56.380
Matthew Swindells: Usually, on this Board

00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:02.520
cycle, I would now be talking about the November
data, but as we published the December Performance

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:08.680
Report at 09.30 this morning, I am going to
talk about the position from December rather

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.710
than what went out originally in the Board
papers, if that is okay with you.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:19.459
Emergency and urgent services
The December figures show that the A&amp;E performance

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:30.620
fell to 86% compliant with the four-hour target
in December, which is obviously some way below

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:36.610
the 95% target, and a long way below where
we would hope to be, so there is considerable

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:41.479
work that we need to do as we come out of
winter to turn that round and put ourselves

00:04:41.479 --> 00:04:51.050
back where we want to be. It is reflective
of the high levels of demand on our emergency

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:57.300
and urgent services, and particularly, of
pressure on beds within hospitals and the

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:03.279
ability to admit, but I think I should start
by just thanking the NHS staff who have worked

00:05:03.279 --> 00:05:10.600
so hard across the winter period, and I think
have gone above and beyond the call of duty,

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:15.389
as they always do when the service is under
pressure, to try and ensure that patient care

00:05:15.389 --> 00:05:19.830
is as good as it possibly can be. Simon said
it earlier, and I would also like to put on

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:30.379
record my thanks to them. We saw emergency
admissions rise by 2.1% in December compared

00:05:30.379 --> 00:05:39.479
to December the year previously, compared
to December 2015. Against that, it is also

00:05:39.479 --> 00:05:49.900
worth noting that A&amp;E attendances per day
fell for the fourth month running, so in December

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:56.879
2016, it was at 63,000 attendances per day,
was the average across December, and if you

00:05:56.879 --> 00:06:02.979
go back to July, it was at 67,000 per day.
I think the message that we have putting out

00:06:02.979 --> 00:06:07.839
to the public, that there are other ways of
accessing urgent services and that A&amp;Es are

00:06:07.839 --> 00:06:13.059
for people who need an A&amp;E, and you can go
to your GP, you can go to an urgent care centre,

00:06:13.059 --> 00:06:18.500
you can ring 111, you can go to a minor injuries
unit, has been heeded by the public, and I

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:25.009
am grateful to the public for that, because
certainly they have helped with the way that

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:32.570
we have been able to handle the December period.
It is also worth noting that the 666,000 people

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:39.589
who used one of those urgent care or walk-in
centres, or minor injuries units, their turnaround

00:06:39.589 --> 00:06:44.490
time for being seen and treated and being
able to leave was 99%, so those services not

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:51.559
only offer good-quality services, but they
are far more convenient for the public if

00:06:51.559 --> 00:06:54.439
they do not need to be in an A&amp;E department.
Primary care

00:06:54.439 --> 00:07:01.789
I would also like to put on record my thanks
to primary care GPs and their teams for the

00:07:01.789 --> 00:07:06.710
way that they have stepped up over the winter
to help relieve pressure, both by GPs going

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:12.770
in and working within A&amp;E departments and
helping see, treat and turn patients around

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:18.469
and get them the right care, but also by extending
their hours to ensure that people could get

00:07:18.469 --> 00:07:26.529
access to the services that they needed, and
I think that is reflected in the very good

00:07:26.529 --> 00:07:32.580
performance for people who need urgent treatment
and are not looking to be admitted, because

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:39.259
the real problem now is around patients who
need to be admitted urgently to hospital,

00:07:39.259 --> 00:07:45.210
and at the back-end of that, our ability to
discharge patients back out of hospital beds.

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:54.680
In December, we saw 195,000 bed days lost
to delayed transfers of care, so that is over

00:07:54.680 --> 00:08:00.460
6,000 NHS beds effectively that have patients
in them who ought to be more appropriately

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:05.649
being cared for somewhere else, and that is
an increase of 1,300 beds on the same position

00:08:05.649 --> 00:08:15.339
in December 2015. Imagine closing one of our
largest hospitals; that is the impact on capacity

00:08:15.339 --> 00:08:20.099
that delayed transfers of care has had. We
saw in December a very small decrease in the

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:25.249
number of those patients waiting because they
needed an NHS service, but the number of patients

00:08:25.249 --> 00:08:30.710
waiting for social care packages or social
care services is a third higher than it had

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:37.300
been a year earlier, so we are really seeing
the impact that that has on services.

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:42.540
Despite all of those pressures, in the elective

00:08:42.550 --> 00:08:54.120
services, we have seen more admitted and non-admitted
waiting list pathways completed in December

00:08:54.120 --> 00:09:00.360
2016 than we did in 2015, and we have, as
Simon said, seen an acceleration of operations,

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:07.490
so more elective operations in the third quarter,
so October, November and December of 2016,

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:17.699
than we did in the first quarter, April, May,
June of 2016. We are still seeing a growth

00:09:17.699 --> 00:09:22.750
in the amount of waiting list activity, and
because of that, we have only seen a very

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:32.170
small reduction in performance around the
18-week target; that fell to 89.7% against

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:41.829
the 92% target. People should just remember,
we say within 18 weeks from seeing your GP,

00:09:41.829 --> 00:09:46.209
being referred into hospital, being seen in
outpatients, having all of your diagnostic

00:09:46.209 --> 00:09:51.300
tests and then receiving your treatment, we
are saying that should happen in 18 weeks,

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:57.780
and for nearly 90% of patients, that does
happen. That is world-class access, by any

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:07.600
standards, so I think it is impressive that
we, under all of this pressure, are still

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:09.959
seeing that work go on.

00:10:09.959 --> 00:10:14.180
I would also just lastly draw your attention
to cancer performance, where we continue to

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:22.089
hit seven of the eight performance standards
for cancer, and we are just below the 85%

00:10:22.089 --> 00:10:27.470
standard for patients starting their treatment
within 62 days. We need to do that last 2%

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:31.589
push to get ourselves over the target, but
the performance is still good.

00:10:31.589 --> 00:10:35.259
I should conclude by reiterating my thanks

00:10:35.259 --> 00:10:40.050
to staff across the board. It is extremely
pressured out there. You can watch it on the

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:44.050
telly, or you can go and visit a hospital;
you can feel just how busy it is and the extents

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:49.699
to which we are keeping the show on the road,
and our staff are working to do that. It is

00:10:49.699 --> 00:10:55.029
fantastic. I would just like to throw it across
to Jane here, who is our Chief Nursing Officer,

00:10:55.029 --> 00:11:00.840
just to say something about the cold weather
alert that has just gone out, and what people

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:03.750
can do to help themselves, help their families
and help their neighbours.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:08.820
Jane Cummings: I just want to reiterate, I

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:14.600
have spent quite a lot of time actually out
in the system, so I have visited communities,

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:20.040
I have been in A&amp;E departments, I have been
talking to staff actually delivering services

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:24.390
at the front line and in the community, and
it is very pressurised. They are working extremely

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:30.550
hard, but still providing in the vast majority
of cases extremely good care. Alongside that,

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:34.240
with all of those pressures, we have just
had a cold weather alert that many people

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:40.120
will be aware of, so the weather for this
weekend until Monday is very cold. We know

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:45.449
that cold weather has a negative impact on
people’s health, particularly those who

00:11:45.449 --> 00:11:52.709
are elderly, frail or have other conditions.
You get an increase in heart attacks, strokes,

00:11:52.709 --> 00:11:58.240
respiratory disease, etc., so actually looking
after yourself during cold weather and helping

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:04.180
relatives and neighbours is really important.
There is well-known advice, which is, try

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:10.610
and keep your house or where you live warm,
to about 18 degrees. Make sure that you eat

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:16.420
regularly, plenty of hot drinks, hot fluids.
That you dress in multiple layers of clothing,

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:22.440
that you stock up with drugs and food, and
neighbours can actually help and keep an eye

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:28.060
on local people, particularly those who are
more vulnerable and elderly. I think that

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:33.069
is really important, just to try and help
manage what we know is always an impact in

00:12:33.069 --> 00:12:37.740
the Health Service, and actually enable people
to stay as well as possible. It is a really,

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:41.750
really important message. Some of the things
that people often say are, ‘Fuel is really

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:47.670
expensive,’ and it is, but there are services
and systems out there – winter fuel payments,

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:51.351
cold weather payments, warm house discounts
– that actually people can access, and I

00:12:51.351 --> 00:12:55.091
encourage people to really do that and to
make sure that families and friends know about that.

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:05.800
Malcolm Grant: I think we receive the report,

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:11.400
and we will understand that continuing to
drive improvement in urgent and emergency

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:16.050
care will be one of the key things that will
occupy our attention over the coming year,

00:13:16.050 --> 00:13:20.509
will be a key point, I am assuming, with the
delivery plan, business plan, and we will

00:13:20.509 --> 00:13:29.149
come back to see how quickly the NHS in England
can get itself back up to the target objectives.

